  "text": "We are given:\n\n$$\n\\arccos (\\cos \\alpha), \\quad \\arccos (\\cos 3 \\alpha), \\quad \\arccos (\\cos 11 \\alpha), \\quad \\arccos (\\cos t \\alpha)\n$$\n\nand the condition that **this sequence is an arithmetic progression** for some **positive** $ \\alpha $ with $ 0 < \\alpha < \\pi $.\n\n---\n\n### Step 1: Understand the structure\n\nLet’s denote the four terms of the arithmetic progression as:\n\n$$\na_1 = \\arccos (\\cos \\alpha), \\quad a_2 = \\arccos (\\cos 3 \\alpha), \\quad a_3 = \\arccos (\\cos 11 \\alpha), \\quad a_4 = \\arccos (\\cos t \\alpha)\n$$\n\nWe are told that this sequence is an arithmetic progression. So:\n\n$$\na_2 - a_1 = a_3 - a_2 = a_4 - a_3\n$$\n\n---\n\n### Step 2: Use the identity for cosine in arccos\n\nRecall:\n\n$$\n\\arccos (\\cos x) = x \\quad \\text{if } x \\in [0, \\pi]\n$$\n\nSo:\n\n- $ \\arccos (\\cos \\alpha) = \\alpha $, since $ 0 < \\alpha < \\pi $\n- $ \\arccos (\\cos 3\\alpha) = 3\\alpha $, since $ 0 < 3\\alpha < 3\\pi $\n- $ \\arccos (\\cos 11\\alpha) = 11\\alpha $, since $ 0 < 11\\alpha < \\pi $ (i.e., $ \\alpha < \\pi /11 $)\n\nSo the four terms become:\n\n$$\na_1 = \\alpha, \\quad a_2 = 3\\alpha, \\quad a_3 = 11\\alpha, \\quad a_4 = t\\alpha\n$$\n\n---\n\n### Step 3: Determine the condition for the sequence to be arithmetic\n\nSince this is an arithmetic progression:\n\n$$\na_2 - a_1 = a_3 - a_2 = a_4 - a_3\n$$\n\nSo:\n\n$$\n3\\alpha - \\alpha = 11\\alpha - 3\\alpha = t\\alpha - 11\\alpha\n$$\n\nCompute:\n\n- $ 3\\alpha - \\alpha = 2\\alpha $\n- $ 11\\alpha - 3\\alpha = 8\\alpha $\n- $ t\\alpha - 11\\alpha = (t - 11)\\alpha $\n\nSet all equal:\n\n$$\n2\\alpha = 8\\alpha = (t - 11)\\alpha\n$$\n\nThis implies:\n\n$$\n2 = 8 = t - 11 \\Rightarrow t = 11\n$$\n\nSo the least positive value of $ t $ is:\n\n$$\n\\boxed{11}\n$$",
